WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.940
A neuron fires most at the peak of its tuning curve.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.840
Is that where it best tells two stimuli apart?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:13.053
Here is a made-up neuron, tuned to the angle of a bar.

4
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:17.573
Its rates are on screen, and each carries noise from trial to trial.

5
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:23.447
Show two nearby angles many times, and draw a histogram of spike counts for each.

6
00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:27.007
Overlapping histograms are hard to tell apart.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.860
What matters is the gap between the means, measured in units of noise.

8
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.840
That gap is called d′.

9
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.840
prime.

10
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:39.473
Compare a pair near the peak with a pair on the steep flank, the same angle apart.

11
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:41.913
Which pair is easier to tell apart?

12
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:53.033
The flank pair. The curve is steeper there, so the means sit about twice as far

13
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:54.093
apart in noise units.

14
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:58.673
The peak is where a neuron fires most, not where it discriminates best.

15
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:01.417
Behaviour has its own curve.

16
00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:05.237
In a two-choice task, guessing gives fifty percent correct.

17
00:01:06.177 --> 00:01:10.657
So fifty percent is chance, and a common threshold is seventy-five percent.

18
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:16.030
Now assume the counts are bell-shaped, independent, and equally noisy for both

19
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.030
stimuli.

20
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:22.870
Then an ideal observer comparing one neuron's counts from two intervals scores by d′

21
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:23.870
prime alone.

22
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:28.243
That gives the neuron a neurometric curve, on the animal's scale.

23
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:32.803
Britten, Shadlen, Newsome and Movshon made that comparison.

24
00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:36.503
Rhesus monkeys judged which way a field of dots drifted.

25
00:01:37.617 --> 00:01:42.877
While a monkey worked, they recorded one neuron in area MT, with the display fitted

26
00:01:42.877 --> 00:01:43.877
to that neuron.

27
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:48.327
From its spike counts on the same trials, they built the neuron's curve.

28
00:01:49.227 --> 00:01:53.147
Under those conditions, most neurons were about as sensitive as the monkey.

29
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.510
So did that one neuron make the monkey's choice?

30
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:06.653
No. It holds about as much signal as the monkey used.

31
00:02:07.413 --> 00:02:08.593
That is signal, not cause.

32
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:11.913
Many neurons respond to the same dots.

33
00:02:12.773 --> 00:02:17.913
A good readout of many similarly informative neurons, with independent noise, could

34
00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:20.073
be far more sensitive than any one of them.

35
00:02:20.853 --> 00:02:22.553
The monkey shows much less improvement.

36
00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:26.387
In the group's model, part of their noise is shared.

37
00:02:27.487 --> 00:02:31.047
Shared noise rises and falls together, so averaging cannot cancel it.

38
00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:34.147
The causal test takes a manipulation.

39
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:38.507
Microstimulating area MT biased the monkeys' choices.

40
00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:43.717
Last, is a taste coded by one line, or by a pattern across many?

41
00:02:44.617 --> 00:02:45.777
That argument continues.

42
00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:48.137
Keep the one idea.

43
00:02:48.137 --> 00:02:51.037
A spike means what its neuron is tuned to.

44
00:02:51.557 --> 00:02:55.657
Small changes are easiest to see where the rate changes a lot against the noise.
